[[Open This|Git Troubleshooting]]

---

''Check the basics''

* `git status` shows your branch, staged/unstaged changes, and any in-progress merge or rebase.
* `git log --oneline --graph --all -20` shows where you and your branches actually are.
* `git diff` and `git diff --staged` show what changed.

''Read the error message.'' Git’s errors usually name the problem and often suggest the fix (e.g., “failed to push some refs” means your remote has commits you don’t).

''Undo common mistakes''

* Bad commit, not pushed: `git reset --soft HEAD~1` (keeps changes) or `git commit --amend`
* Discard local edits to a file: `git restore <file>`
* Unstage: `git restore --staged <file>`
* Safe undo of a pushed commit: `git revert <sha>`

''Use the safety net: git reflog.'' It records every place HEAD has been, so “lost” commits after a bad reset, rebase, or deleted branch are usually recoverable with `git reset --hard <sha>` or `git checkout -b rescue <sha>`.

''Get out of stuck states''

* Merge conflict: edit the marked files, git add them, then git commit (or git merge --abort to bail).
* Rebase mess: git rebase --abort
* Detached HEAD: git switch -c new-branch to keep your work.

''Diagnose config/connection problems''

git config --list --show-origin shows where settings come from.
GIT_TRACE=1 git <command> or GIT_CURL_VERBOSE=1 git push shows what’s happening under the hood.
Auth issues: check git remote -v, your credential helper, and SSH keys (ssh -T git@github.com).